    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Punch-marked silver and the market superintendent",
    text: "From about the sixth century BCE the cities of the Ganga plain paid in karshapanas: small bent bars and then flat pieces of silver stamped with punches, a sun, a hill, a tree, a six-armed wheel, each punch added by an issuing authority or a money-tester. The Buddhist Jatakas are full of setthis, great merchants, and of caravans. The Arthashastra, a statecraft manual of the Maurya age, sets a panyadhyaksha over commerce: weights and measures checked every four months, prices fixed on imports and local goods with a set margin for the seller, fines for short measure and adulteration. Most people still bought little with coin; grain, cloth and labour changed hands in kind.",
