    text: "Craftsmen of the early historic cities organised in shrenis, guilds under an elder (jetthaka) with their own rules, which kings were told to respect. The Jatakas speak of villages of a thousand carpenters and of smiths' streets. At the Nasik caves, around 120 CE, the Saka general Ushavadata deposited 3,000 karshapanas with two weavers' guilds, one paying interest at one per cent a month, the other at three-quarters, the interest to clothe the monks. The guild was a bank, a court and an insurer. At Sanchi an inscription on the south gateway credits the ivory-carvers of Vidisha with carving it. Sons learned in the father's workshop; outsiders could join by apprenticeship.",
