    text: "The Arthashastra gives the state's weaving superintendent, the sutradhyaksha, a job that is also a welfare scheme. He was to have wool, bark fibre, cotton, hemp and flax spun by widows, disabled women, unmarried girls, women who had left home, and women working off fines, paying them by the fineness and amount of yarn. Women who could not come out of the house were to send their maidservants to fetch fibre and return the yarn, and he was to deal with them in half light, without staring at them, on pain of punishment. How far any king ran such workshops is uncertain, but the text shows spinning as the fallback living of women without a husband.",
