    text: "The great road of the north, the Uttarapatha, ran from Taxila through Mathura to Pataliputra and on to the Bengal coast; the Dakshinapatha went south to the Deccan. Caravans of bullock carts moved under a sarthavaha, a caravan leader who hired guides, guards and water-finders; the Jatakas tell of caravans lost in deserts when the water gave out. Ashoka had banyan trees planted along the roads for shade, and wells and rest houses dug every half-kos, as his seventh pillar edict boasts. The Ganga carried heavier loads, and ferrymen at every crossing charged tolls the Arthashastra set by load. Porters carried headloads on the stretches carts could not reach.",
