    title: "Terracotta ring wells in the early cities",
    text: "In the crowded cities of the Ganga plain, Mathura, Kaushambi, Pataliputra, Ujjain, people sank shafts lined with stacked rings of fired clay, each a hand's breadth deep and a metre or so across. Some reached groundwater and were wells; others were soakage pits for household waste, and archaeologists sometimes argue over which. They are so common in early historic layers that they are almost a period marker. Where the ground was stony, people drew from stepped tanks and from the river. Carrying water in pots on the head or hip was women's daily work, and the Buddhist texts set many meetings, including monks asking for a drink, at the village well.",
