    text: "Religious life in the age of the Buddha and Ashoka was plural. Villagers honoured yakshas and nagas, spirits of trees, pools and places, with flowers, lamps and food at shrines under great trees; huge stone yakshas stood in Mathura and Patna. Buddhists and Jains built stupas over relics and circled them clockwise, and the inscriptions at Bharhut and Sanchi name hundreds of lay donors, many of them women, who paid for a railing pillar or a crossbar. Brahmins still performed sacrifices for kings and householders. A family could give alms to a Buddhist monk, feed a Brahmin and leave milk at a naga's anthill in the same week without contradiction.",
