    text: "Tamil poets of the Sangam age divided the world into five tinai, landscapes each with its people, gods and moods. The seashore, neytal, belonged to the paratavar, fishing folk who went out on log rafts, kattumaram, the ancestor of the catamaran, with nets and lines, and whose women dried fish and sold it inland. The same shore had salt pans, and the umanar, salt merchants, drove carts of salt slowly into the interior with their families aboard, bartering it measure for measure against paddy. Poems of this landscape are poems of waiting: a woman watching the sea for a lover's boat. Pearl divers worked the Gulf of Mannar for the Pandya kings.",
