    text: "The mullai tinai, the scrubby forest pasture, belonged to herders, the ayar and kovalar, who played flutes and kept cattle and goats. Cattle raids were the opening move of war, and men died defending or seizing herds. The dead man was given a nadukal, a planted stone with his name and deed, often his picture with bow and shield, and from the fourth century BCE the stones carry Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions. Sangam poems describe people decking them with peacock feathers, pouring toddy and offering to them as to gods. Hero stones went on being raised in the south for more than two thousand years, for men killed by tigers and in feuds as well as raids.",
