    text: "From the Gupta age on, kings gave villages to Brahmins and temples by charter, and the charter was engraved on copper plates strung on a ring with the royal seal, for the grantee to keep. The text lists the boundaries by trees, tanks and anthills, the taxes remitted, usually the king's share of grain, a sixth or so, plus dues, and warns that anyone who takes it back will be born as a worm in dung for sixty thousand years. Writing and keeping these was the work of scribes (karanika, divira), and it is in this era that the scribal Kayasthas appear. The village headman, gramika, witnessed the grant and still collected what was owed.",
