    text: "Most of the Tamil country has no great river flood, so villages lived by the eri: an earth embankment across a slope that caught the monsoon rain, with stone sluices to let water onto the paddy below. The village assembly, the ur or, in Brahmin villages, the sabha, managed it. At Uttaramerur, inscriptions of 919 and 921 CE record how members of the tank committee, the eri-variyam, were to be chosen by lot from eligible landholders, who had to own land, have a house and not have fallen into certain crimes. Tank silt had to be dug out each dry season, and grants of land were made to pay for it. Hereditary water turners, later called neerkatti, opened the sluices by turn.",
