    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Sheep that neither die nor age",
    text: "When Rajaraja Chola finished the great temple at Thanjavur around 1010, he and his court endowed perpetual lamps, and inscriptions on the temple walls record how. For each lamp 96 ewes, or 48 cows, or 16 buffaloes were handed to named shepherds in the villages around, who in return had to bring a fixed measure of ghee to the temple every day, for ever. The phrase is chava-muva-peradu, sheep that neither die nor grow old: the shepherd had to replace losses from the flock's own increase. Shepherd families and their descendants were bound to the lamp, and the herder's surplus fed the temple's light.",
