    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Casting a Chola bronze",
    text: "The famous Chola bronzes, Shiva dancing as Nataraja, Parvati, the saints, were made by lost-wax casting. The sthapati modelled the whole figure solid in beeswax mixed with resin, following iconometric proportions measured in finger-widths from the face; covered it in layers of fine clay from the Kaveri bank; heated it so the wax ran out; and poured in molten copper alloy. The mould was broken, so every image is unique, and then chased and polished for weeks. The final act was the opening of the eyes, after which the image was a god. Families of casters at Swamimalai near Thanjavur still work this way and trace their craft to Chola patronage.",
