    title: "The Five Hundred of Ayyavole",
    text: "Long-distance trade in the medieval south was run by merchant bodies. The greatest called itself the Five Hundred Lords of Ayyavole, after its base in Karnataka, and spread through the Tamil lands, Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia, with armed guards, its own flag and a eulogy recited in its inscriptions. The Manigramam was another. In each town the nagaram, the merchants' assembly, ran the market. A Tamil inscription of 1088 at Barus in Sumatra records the Ayyavole merchants there, buying camphor and resins. Cloth, pepper, horses, pearls and areca moved through their hands; the Chola raid on Srivijaya in 1025 was probably made in their interest.",
