    title: "Cutting granite for Pallava and Chola temples",
    text: "At Mamallapuram in the seventh century, Pallava masons carved whole temples, the rathas, and a vast relief out of standing granite outcrops, working down from the top with iron chisels and hammers. Unfinished pieces show the method: a line of wedge slots cut along the grain, wedges driven in, the block split off. Later the Cholas built free-standing granite temples of enormous size; the tower of Thanjavur rises some sixty metres, capped by a stone that tradition says was dragged up a ramp from a village miles away. Designers, sthapatis, worked from the Shilpa texts, and masons were hereditary; the caste of Kammalar grouped stone, wood and metal workers together.",
