    text: "A great Chola temple was a town's employer. The Thanjavur inscriptions list its staff by name and pay in paddy: priests, drummers, singers of Tamil hymns, accountants, lamp-lighters, washermen, potters, carpenters, astrologers and some four hundred temple women, tali-cheri pendir, brought from other temples and housed in two streets. These women, later called devadasis, danced and sang in the daily rites and festivals and held land in their own right. Ordinary worshippers came at the fixed times of puja to see the deity, bring flowers and oil, and hear the Tevaram hymns. The festivals, when the god's bronze image was carried round the streets, drew in the whole countryside.",
