    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Tanks, sluices and the village wewa",
    text: "In Sri Lanka's dry zone, every village sat below a wewa, a reservoir held by an earthen bund, and the classic landscape was tank, paddy field and dagoba. From about the third century BCE the kings of Anuradhapura built giant tanks and feeder canals too: the Kala Wewa fed the Jaya Ganga, which drops only about ten centimetres per kilometre over nearly ninety kilometres. The key invention was the bisokotuwa, a valve pit behind the sluice that let water out without the pressure destroying the bund. Parakramabahu I in the twelfth century built the Parakrama Samudra and is said to have ordered that not one drop of rain reach the sea without serving man.",
