    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Chena plots in the dry zone",
    text: "Alongside the tank paddy, almost every dry-zone household cut a chena, hena in Sinhala: a patch of scrub forest felled in the dry months, burned just before the rains, and sown with finger millet (kurakkan), other millets, maize after the Portuguese, sesame, pumpkins and chillies, then left to regrow for years. It was the family's fallback when the tank failed. The worst task was watching: a man sat nights in a pela, a hut on stilts, shouting and beating drums against elephants, pigs and deer. The British Crown Lands Encroachment Ordinance of 1840 and later forest rules claimed uncleared land for the state and pressed hard on chena.",
