    title: "Rajakariya: service owed to the king",
    text: "In the Kandyan kingdom land was held in return for service, rajakariya, and the service followed caste: cultivators served as soldiers and bearers, smiths made weapons, potters supplied pots, washers laid white cloth for processions, drummers drummed at the Temple of the Tooth. Each village had its headmen who called people out and kept count. Service could mean weeks away repairing tanks, carrying loads for officials or cutting roads, and it was a heavy burden in the planting season. The British used it for road building after 1815 until the Colebrooke-Cameron reforms of 1832 abolished compulsory service, which then helped push the Kandyan peasants off the land planters wanted.",
